purchases are deferrable, and 16% procedure growth from a high base is not sustainable.\n5. Recurring revenue growth is tied to procedure volumes which face reimbursement pressure if CMS/Medicaid policy shifts.\n6. SBC of $0.788B/year [Actual] is a real cash cost; the market's vendor FCF of $3.2B TTM overstates owner earnings by ~25%.\n7. The 38% drawdown from $604 to $375 may reflect informed selling of a crowded institutional holding (89.7% institutional ownership [Actual]) \u2014 no contrarian signal yet.\nStep 9-2 Logic Combat:\nLong case strength: 8/10 (fortress moat, real growth, best-in-class margins).\nShort case strength: 7/10 (valuation + competition + regulatory).\nGap = 1 \u21d2 CONVICTION DOWNGRADE, consistent with 0% position.\nStep 9-3 Adversarial Data:\n- FDA stapler communication [Estimate \u2014 Yahoo Finance article, July 2026].\n- \"Fraud by design\" claim re: robotic mastectomy FDA loophole [Unconfirmed \u2014 Medium article; not litigated, treat as unverified].\nStep 9-4 Killed Arguments:\n- \"The stock has already fallen 38%, so it must be cheap\": KILLED \u2014 price 375 is still 51% above IV base.\n- \"10,000+ installed base makes competition irrelevant\": KILLED \u2014 it makes switching costly, but hospital buyers can add a second robot; J&J/Medtronic do not need to displace da Vinci to compress ISRG's pricing.\n- \"Analysts say $478 average target\": KILLED \u2014 sell-side targets lag price and were set before the FDA stapler news.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n--------------------------------\nA. Methodology Audit: PASS \u2014 Engine 3 selected with j