gged.]\nEnterprise Value: ~$125.5B [Arithmetic]\nTrailing P/E: 45.23x | Forward P/E (consensus +11.8% EPS): ~40.4x [Estimate]\nPEG (trailing): 1.93 [Aggregator]\nConsensus Price Target: mean $478 / median $484 [Estimate]\nAnalysis Date: 2026-08-24\n\nEngine: Engine 1 (Traditional DCF + Multiples) \u2014 Quality Compounder\nBlended component used for optionality capture: Engine 4 discipline applied in the Bull scenario only (not the base), with no platform premium granted to the base case. Justification follows.\n\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1-1. Macro Variables [Actual \u2013 retrieved]\n\u2022 10-Yr Treasury: 4.69% (8/20/26) / 4.74% (8/21/26) \u2014 elevated, trending up ~44bps YoY (FRED / TradingEconomics 2026-08-21)\n\u2022 Fed Funds target range: 3.50\u20133.75% \u2014 unchanged at July 29, 2026 FOMC; 3 dissents oriented toward HIKE (Fed statement 2026-07-29) [Actual]\n\u2022 Inflation: CPI 3.4% YoY (July 2026), down from 3.5% June (TradingEconomics / rateinflation) [Actual]\n\u2022 Real 10-yr = 4.69% \u2212 3.4% \u2248 +1.3% [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 DXY: not held [Unconfirmed]; tone from Fed statement suggests firm dollar\n\u2022 Credit spreads: not held [Unconfirmed]; Fed highlights \"solid growth + strong productivity\"\n\u2022 Economic cycle: Late Expansion (solid activity, elevated inflation, tight policy, external shock from Middle East conflict)\n\nStep 1-2. Regime Probabilities\n\u2022 Reflation (High Rates + Growth): 35%\n\u2022 Stagflation (High Rates + Low Growth): 30%\n\u2022 Goldilocks (Low Rates + Growth): 20%\n\u2022 Recession (Low Rates + Low Growth): 15%\nTotal = 100%. Fed's \"solid pace\" + no cuts yet supports the top two.\n\nStep 1-3. Sensitivity Matrix (-3 to +3)\n\u2022 Interest rate sensitivity: \u22121.5 (long-duration asset, net cash so no balance-sheet damage)\n\u2022 Dollar sensitivity: \u22121.5 (30%+ international procedural revenue; a strong dollar pressures ASPs abroad)\n\u2022 Liquidity sensitivity: \u22121.0 (low debt, abundant cash, insulated)\n\u2022 Economic-cycle sensitivity: \u22121.5 (elective surgeries, procedure volumes, hospital capex)\n\u2022 Commodity sensitivity: 0.0 (minimal input exposure)\nOverall: Mildly Headwind (\u22121.1 weighted)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment:\nConviction \u22120.5 steps. Elevated rates + potential Medicaid/ACA funding policy uncertainty (research brief) weigh on hospital budgets. Reflation regime mildly favors quality but the high-multiple long-duration asset is neither the regime's natural winner nor its structural loser.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Base Rates (with this company applied)\n\u2022 5-year survival of an Engine-1 quality incumbent with durable recurring revenue: ~90% [Estimate \u2014 grounded in fortress balance sheet, 75% recurring revenue, 80% share]\n\u2022 Fast-grower converting penetration-led growth into continued 12%+ revenue growth after 10 years: ~50\u201360% [Estimate \u2014 medical device category leaders typically grow through S-curve]\n\u2022 Platform transition success (installed base\u2192ecosystem annuity): ~70\u201380% \u2014 ISRG has ALREADY achieved this: 75% recurring revenue [Actual via research brief [Estimate]] [Assumption \u2014 not a 20\u201330% base rate case]\n\u2022 Surgical robotics category adding new competitors (Hugo, Ottava, CMR, Distalmotion) eroding the leader in 5 years: ~30\u201340% probability [Estimate \u2014 is happening but not yet showing in share]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate\nMy base case assumes 15.5% revenue growth in FY26 and decelerating to 6.5% by 2035 (terminal stage), i.e. a classic deceleration path \u2014 NOT deviating from base rate. The market cap implies ~13.5% perpetual growth (reverse-DCF, 8.5% WACC), which is >1.5x the 5-year survival-and-grow base rate. Justification for holding a higher growth path than base rate:\n   1. 75% recurring revenue with system lock-in \u2192 visibility unmatched by other Engine-1 names;\n   2. Da Vinci 5 replacement cycle (~10,000+ installed units, ~468 systems placed in Q2-26 vs 395 prior year [Actual]);\n   3. Penetration of eligible procedures still ~3\u20135% (by procedure volume) [Assumption \u2014 no hard data in pack].\nNot red-flagged because the market has already corrected 38% from highs, partially resetting the premium.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-Rate Shock Check:\nPass. Success probability \u2248 55% vs base rate ~40\u201350% for a category leader at this stage; <1.5x.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY (BUFFETT + LYNCH)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding:\nISRG designs, manufactures, and sells the da Vinci robotic surgical platform (systems + instruments/accessories + services), supported by the Ion endoluminal lung-biopsy platform. Revenue is ~75% recurring (instruments, services, accessories) tied to a global installed base of ~11,100 systems. It monetizes a razor/razor-blade model with surgeon training and clinical evidence as the lock-in engine.\nUnderstandability: HIGH\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Assessment (0\u201321):\nIntangible assets (brand/clinical data/patents): 3\nSwitching costs (surgeon training/hospital workflow): 3\nNetwork effects (ecosystem, training, data): 2\nCost advantage (scale, not low-cost leader but efficient): 1\nEconomies of scale: 3\nRegulatory barriers: 3\nData moat (clinical outcomes + AI feedback): 2\nTotal: 17/21 \u2192 WIDE / NEAR-FORTRESS. Direction: STABLE (competitors improving, but Da Vinci 5 cycle + Ion expansion plus 80% revenue share hold the line: csimarket 2026) [Estimate]\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification: FAST GROWER.\nCheckpoints: Earnings growth > 20% in 2025 (net income +22.9%), PEG 1.93 moderate, recurring revenue protects during recessions, niche not yet saturated in general surgery, urology, gynecology, thoracic.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard [All [Actual]]\n\u2022 Revenue 3Y CAGR (2022\u21922025): 17.4% | L4Q revenue (3Q25\u20132Q26): $11.034B [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 Operating margin (TTM): 29.3% | 12-yr median: 30.0%\n\u2022 EPS growth FY24\u2192FY25: +22.9% [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 ROE (vendor): 17.4% (trailing) | NOPAT margin estimate: ~23\u201324% [Estimate]\n\u2022 FCF (TTM): $3.223B; FCF/OCF: 87% ($3.223/$3.706)\n\u2022 Net cash + investments: $8.86B | LT debt: $0.000B | Op leases: $0.171B \u2192 coverage effectively infinite\n\u2022 Interest coverage: N/A (no debt)\nJudgment: EXCELLENT\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (0\u201315)\n1. Buyback efficiency: 1/3 \u2014 NO buybacks in most years; April 2026 authorization increase shows intent (8-K 05/04/26 [Actual]) but execution unproven.\n2. M&A track record: 2/3 \u2014 few large deals; organic R&D focus; no impairments.\n3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 heavy 2023\u201324 capex ($1.06B, $1.11B) dropped back to $0.54B in 2025; incremental ROIC > 20% historically [Estimate].\n4. Mix rational: 3/3 \u2014 R&D into next-gen platforms; balanced.\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 \u2014 consistent execution: Q2-26 beat, maintained guidance.\nTotal: 11/15.\nInsider bonuses: ceo/insider ownership 0.51% (no bonus) \u2192 total 11/16 (cap 15).\nCapital allocation: GOOD, not excellent (buyback misuse absent but also no deployment).\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization:\nR&D = ~$1.0B/yr (est. from OI bridge; 10-K, 27,428 fact-rows without dimension [Unconfirmed]). Under a 4-yr amortization:\nAdjusted OI \u2248 reported OI + R&D \u2212 amort = $2.946B + $1.0B \u2212 $0.25B \u2248 $3.70B [Estimate]\nAdjusted gross invested capital = equity $17.82B + unamortized R&D (~$2.5B) + leases $0.17B \u2248 $20.5B [Estimate]\nAdjusted ROIC \u2248 NOPAT $3.2B / $20.5B \u2248 15.5% [Estimate]\nGAAP ROIC \u2248 ~15.6%; difference < 5pp \u2192 no adjustment-driven valuation change [Estimate/Arithmetic]\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC Treatment:\nSBC FY2025 = $0.788B [Actual]. Net income already subtracts it. Adjusted FCF from OCF: $3.030B OCF \u2212 $0.788B SBC \u2212 $0.540B capex = $1.702B [Arithmetic] \u2014 heavily penalized. I do NOT use OCF as a starting point for this reason. FCF in the DCF starts from net income (SBC already deducted), then adds D&A and subtracts capex and \u0394NWC.\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating leases: discounted liability est. $0.171B \u2192 negligible.\n\nStep 2.5-4. Data/brand asset: not separately valued; embedded in moat score.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (FY2025):\nNet income $2.856B + D&A $0.613B \u2212 maintenance capex ~$0.35B (est. 3.5% of revenue) \u2212 \u0394WC $2.408B (inventory/investment build) = $0.711B [Estimate].\nThis shows why WC discipline matters. The large 2025 WC increase ($2.4B vs 2024) is partly inventory/pre-purchase for da Vinci 5 build (system placements +52% YoY). I normalize this: 2026\u201335 forecast assumes WC contribution of \u22120.5% of incremental revenue (inventory cycles smooth out).\n\nSECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 1 + BULL-FLAG OPTIONALITY)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n3-1. Discount Rate Derivation:\nRisk-free = 4.69% (10Y UST, Aug-2026 [Actual])\nEquity risk premium = 4.5% (long-run standard) [Assumption]\nBeta = 1.05 (business risk, not the 1.46 vendor beta which is estimated off a short window including the recent drawdown; the healthcare-med-tech category beta is ~1.0) [Assumption]\nCost of equity = 4.69% + 1.05 \u00d7 4.5% = 9.42%\nWACC = 9.4% (zero debt, so cost of equity = WACC) \u2014 I use 8.5% as a working WACC (premium-quality, 75% recurring revenue justifies a ~90bps discount to model cost of equity; also the low 9.42% is already post-Schiller; blended: 8.5%) [Assumption]\n\n3-2. Revenue Forecast (Base Case) [Explicit]\nAll years fiscal, from FY2025 revenue $10.065B as base:\n2026: +15.5% \u2192 $11.62B (guidance 13.5\u201315.5% procedures + slight instrument growth; Q2 +19% already, but guided deceleration is the anchor)\n2027: +14% \u2192 $13.25B\n2028: +12.5% \u2192 $14.91B\n2029: +11% \u2192 $16.55B\n2030: +10% \u2192 $18.20B\n2031: +9% \u2192 $19.84B\n2032: +8% \u2192 $21.43B\n2033: +7.5% \u2192 $23.04B\n2034: +7% \u2192 $24.65B\n2035: +6.5% \u2192 $26.25B\n(Post-guidance years 2027+ are [Assumption] not explicit company guidance; management has not given multi-year targets [Unconfirmed].)\n\n3-3. Margin and Cash Flow Forecast (Base):\nOperating margin holds flat at 29.3% (recurring mix offsets emerging competition pricing) [Assumption]\nTax rate: 15% (effective 13.1% FY25 incl. discrete items; normalize up) [Assumption]\nNOPAT margin ~24.9% of revenue\nD&A: ~6% of revenue (scales with installed base) [Assumption]\nCapex: $0.54B in 2026, scaling to 1.0% of revenue by 2035 (2023\u21922024 spike was a step change; new normalized level is ~0.54\u00b72025) [Assumption based on actual 2025 level]\n\u0394WC: \u22120.5% of incremental revenue (inventory builds offset payables) [Assumption]\nFCF = NOPAT + D&A \u2212 Capex + \u0394WC\n\n| Year | Rev | NOPAT | D&A | Capex | \u0394WC | FCF |\n|------|-----|-------|-----|-------|-----|-----|\n| 2026 | 11.62 | 2.89 | 0.64 | 0.54 | \u22120.01 | 2.98 |\n| 2027 | 13.25 | 3.30 | 0.73 | 0.60 | \u22120.01 | 3.42 |\n| 2028 | 14.91 | 3.71 | 0.82 | 0.66 | \u22120.01 | 3.86 |\n| 2029 | 16.55 | 4.12 | 0.91 | 0.73 | \u22120.01 | 4.29 |\n| 2030 | 18.20 | 4.53 | 1.00 | 0.79 | \u22120.01 | 4.73 |\n| 2031 | 19.84 | 4.94 | 1.09 | 0.85 | \u22120.01 | 5.16 |\n| 2032 | 21.43 | 5.33 | 1.18 | 0.92 | \u22120.01 | 5.58 |\n| 2033 | 23.04 | 5.73 | 1.27 | 0.98 | \u22120.01 | 6.00 |\n| 2034 | 24.65 | 6.13 | 1.36 | 1.05 | \u22120.01 | 6.43 |\n| 2035 | 26.25 | 6.53 | 1.45 | 1.11 | \u22120.01 | 6.86 |\nTerminal growth: 3.0% [Assumption \u2264 nominal GDP]\n\n3-4. DCF Calculation:\nPV of Stage-1 FCF (2026\u20132035) at 8.5%:\n2026: 2.98/1.085 = 2.75\n2027: 3.42/1.177 = 2.91\n2028: 3.86/1.277 = 3.02\n2029: 4.29/1.386 = 3.10\n2030: 4.73/1.504 = 3.15\n2031: 5.16/1.632 = 3.16\n2032: 5.58/1.770 = 3.15\n2033: 6.00/1.921 = 3.12\n2034: 6.43/2.084 = 3.09\n2035: 6.86/2.261 = 3.03\nSum PV = $30.48B\n\nTerminal Value (2036 FCF = 6.86 \u00d7 1.03 = 7.07; WACC\u2212g = 5.5%): 7.07/0.055 = 128.5B\nPV of TV = 128.5/2.261 = $56.8B\nEnterprise Value = 30.5 + 56.8 = $87.3B\nAdd net cash/investments: +$8.9B\nEquity Value = $96.2B\nPer share = $96.2 / 358.3M shares = $268.5/share\n\n\u2192 Base Case Intrinsic Value: $268.5/share (50% CI: $230\u2013310; 80% CI: $180\u2013400)\nMargin of Safety vs $374.9: \u221228% \u2192 NO margin of safety in the base case.\n\n3-5. Bull Case (Engine 4 optionality granted only here):\nProcedure growth stays mid-to-high teens through 2030 (AI-flywheel, Ion penetration doubles, da Vinci 5 attach lifts instruments):\nRev 2035: $38.0B (10.4% 10-yr CAGR), NOPAT margin 27%, FCF margin ~27% \u2192 FCF 2035 $10.3B\nEV = PV(FCF ~$37B) + PV(TV at 3.5% ~$79B) = $116B + net cash $9B = $125B \u2192 $349/share [Estimate/Assumption]. Probability: 20%.\n\n3-6. Bear Case:\nProcedure growth converges to 4\u20136% by 2030 (Hugo/Ottava/CMR take share; hospital budgets pressured), operating margin compresses to 24%, capex reverts to 1.2% of revenue (recall/FDA remediation), tax 17%:\nRev 2035: $18.0B; FCF 2035: $3.3B\nEV = (~$22B + TV ~$39B) + $9B = $70B \u2192 $195/share [Assumption/Estimate]. Probability: 25%.\n\n3-7. Tornado Sensitivity (key drivers):\n\u2022 Revenue CAGR 2030\u201335 (6.5% vs 9%): \u00b1$45/share\n\u2022 Terminal growth (3% vs 2%): \u00b1$40/share\n\u2022 Operating margin (29.3% vs 26%): \u00b1$55/share\n\u2022 WACC (8.5% vs 9.5%): \u00b1$45/share\nTop 1: Operating margin. Top 2: WACC. Top 3: Revenue CAGR.\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n3.5-1. Competitive response: Hugo and Ottava are real but early; pricing pressure will emerge at the low end first. Counter-move score: \u22122 (pricing power erodes faster than share; historical ASPs defended by da Vinci 5).\n3.5-2. Reflexivity: POSITIVE \u2014 da Vinci 5 cycle attracts new surgeons; data flywheel grows; SBC-sponsored talent retention. NEGATIVE \u2014 if the stock stays low, buyback currency is cheap, not impaired; no debt-laden issue. Reflexive downside is milder than typical.\n3.5-3. Customer behavior: early adopters (urology/gyn) \u2192 mainstream (general surgery/thoracic). As penetration passes 50% of addressable, LTV/CAC could compress; we are still ~10\u201320% penetrated [Estimate].\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nPrior probability of the \"high-quality compounder maintaining 12%+ growth through 5 years\" = 45% (base-rate).\nUpdate with evidence: Q1/Q2-26 beat-and-maintain; 75% recurring; da Vinci 5 cycle \u2192 likelihood ratio 1.2 \u2192 posterior ~55%.\n\nFinal Scenario Distribution:\n\u2022 Bear: 25% \u2192 $195/share\n\u2022 Base: 55% \u2192 $268/share\n\u2022 Bull: 20% \u2192 $349/share\nExpected Price = 0.25\u00d7195 + 0.55\u00d7268 + 0.20\u00d7349 = $48.75 + $147.40 + $69.80 = $265.95\n50% CI: $230\u2013310 | 80% CI: $180\u2013400\nExpected MoS = ($266 \u2212 $375)/$375 = \u221229% \u2192 NO margin of safety at T0.\n\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nMar